  "variation": {
    "seeded": true,
    "notes": "The rock's shape and seams, its approach angle and heading, the trail puffs, the debris throw, the smoke and the crater texture change per cast. The warning circle's size, the 1.15 s telegraph and the impact time stay fixed so the dodge window is always fair."
  },
  "director_notes": "Built from the one-word request \"meteor\", as a generic falling-star strike, not any particular game's version. Choices: (1) a long, readable telegraph: a red warning circle sized to the hit plus a ring closing in on the centre, pulsing faster as the hit nears, with the ground light turning orange as the meteor approaches, because a huge hit needs a fair dodge window; (2) the meteor is an orb with look meteor, anchored in the sky with travel projectile, so it falls from above the target at a tilt (approach_deg) from a seeded heading, accelerating (ease_in) over 0.7 s; the rock is a lumpy mesh with molten seams and a white-hot leading face, shedding flame puffs that cool hot to red and a slower smoke trail that hangs in the air after it lands (it reads well from the side and 3/4 views, and as a short streak from the top); (3) the impact stacks a screen flash, a local flare, a fireball (additive smoke), a fast shockwave ring, a dust wave rolling out along the ground (smoke with spread_speed), glowing debris chunks that land and stay (sparks look debris), a spark burst, rising embers and a smoke column; heavy shake on the hit and a faint tremor during the fall; (4) the ground keeps the story: crater (alpha, outlives the spell) under a wide scorch, glowing cracks and a hot burn that cool fast. Sound: a low rumble under the telegraph, a high rushing wind, fire and a late whoosh as it falls, then a deep boom with a crash of debris, and a quiet fire tail. Fire element because the reader's first idea of a meteor is a fireball from the sky; an earth-flavoured variant would swap the palette toward stone and drop the fire trail."
}
